 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**No, the Aussie dollar isn't weak against the yen. It's strong by historical standards. It has eased a bit over the past six weeks, though.**\n\n**Where it is now:** A$1 bought **¥109.67** at the RBA's last reading on **16 October 2026**.\n\n**Recent moves (RBA data):**\n- **Peak:** It hit ¥114.28 at the end of April 2026, and was **¥114.63 on 1 September 2026**.\n- **Since then:** It has fallen about **4%**, from ¥114.63 to ¥109.67. It has stayed between roughly ¥109.4 and ¥110.7 throughout October so far.\n- **A year ago:** It was ¥100.84 at the end of October 2025, so it's up about **9%** on a year earlier.\n- **The low point:** It was ¥91.43 at the end of April 2025, so it's up about **20%** since then.\n- **Since 2023:** For most of 2023 it sat between about ¥89 and ¥98. It only went above ¥110 in 2026, when it was briefly that high in February and stayed there from April to September.\n\n**Longer term:** The data I can access only starts in 2023, so this part comes from general knowledge rather than the figures above. Before now, the Aussie usually peaked against the yen at around ¥105–107, in 2007 and 2013. Anything above about ¥100 has generally been seen as strong. At about ¥110, it's still near multi-decade highs.\n\n**What it means for you:** If you're going to Japan or buying Japanese goods, your dollar still goes a long way, though slightly less far than in August or early September. If the September dip looked like a sign of weakness, it was only a pullback from very high levels. I can't say which way it will go next.",
